The Mercedes-Benz Vito 120 CDI is part of the W639 generation, produced from 2003 to 2014. It is powered by a 3.0-litre V6 turbo-diesel engine (OM642) producing 150 kW (201 hp) and 440 Nm (325 lb-ft) of torque. It is typically paired with a 5-speed automatic transmission and rear-wheel drive.

Acceleration from 0-100 km/h (0-62 mph) is claimed to be around 8.3 seconds by some sources, while others suggest 0-60 mph in 8.0 seconds or around 9.0-9.5 seconds. Top speed is around 195-200 km/h (121-124 mph). Fuel consumption is reported to average around 9.2 L/100km (approx. 25.5 mpg) combined, though some owners report lower figures, particularly in urban driving. CO2 emissions are listed as 237 g/km or 244 g/km.

The W639 Vito comes in various lengths (4748mm to 5223mm) and wheelbases (3200mm or 3430mm). It is available in panel van, crew cab, and passenger (Wagon/Traveliner) body styles, offering seating for up to 9 people and substantial cargo space (5.2 to 6.6 cubic metres depending on configuration). Ground clearance is around 146mm (5.7 inches). Turning circle is around 11.8 to 12.5 meters (38.7 to 41 feet).

Safety features on the W639 Vito included standard ABS, EBD, BAS, ESP, and traction control. Driver airbags were standard, with passenger and side/curtain airbags often optional. When optioned with side head-protecting airbags, the W639 Vito/Viano could achieve a 5-star ANCAP safety rating. ADAS features were limited compared to modern vehicles, though some models offered PARKTRONIC (parking sensors) and adaptive ESP.

Interior features were functional and durable, typical of a commercial vehicle, though passenger versions offered more comfort. Features like air conditioning, power windows, cruise control, and a multi-function steering wheel were available. Infotainment was basic, typically a standard radio, though aftermarket Android units with larger screens, CarPlay/Android Auto, and even ADAS/360 camera support are available for this generation.

Reliability of the OM642 V6 engine is generally considered decent, but some common issues like oil cooler leaks and swirl flap problems have been reported. The 5-speed automatic transmission (722.6) is generally robust but requires regular fluid changes. Some owners reported early issues with engine management or transmission on the 120 CDI, though many were resolved with software updates or minor repairs.

